A||||21||80's BOY||Supercharger boost reccomendations||||5||
Z||000000||80's BOY||07-20-2000||03:01 PM||||How many #s of boost should be applied to a 3.1 stroker?  i plan to use a Paxton SN-90 which puts 9# on a 350 small block or mustang 5.0, but about 13# on a 6cyl. (says Paxton sales rep) would this be too much? plus, ducting is an issue. i need some good feedback on this.....i don't want my 3.1 to detonate.||||reg||5
Z||000001||elfiero||07-20-2000||03:20 PM||||I'd go with paxton on this one,remember-<BR>your knock sensor will stop anything bad<BR>from happening.||||reg||1
Z||000002||BBBAD GT||07-20-2000||03:21 PM||||Thats great that you are trying a centrifugal blower on a V6.  Are you using the old AC bracket or something custom?  I would definately start low on boost.  I would look around to see if you can find a larger blower pulley (they should be out there, check the Mustang classifies).  Also, your crank pulley that drives the blower will play a roll in how many rpms you are spinning the blower (ie boost with these type of blowers).  If you have the diameter of both the blower and crank pulley, you can figure out how fast the blower is spinning (you need to know this).  Also, how are you going to get more fuel in there?  Bigger injectors/custom chip/FMU?  How are you going to get the timing retarded? MSD BTM or custom chip?  <P>One thing about the older Paxtons (namely the ball-drive units), you have to change the 'blower fluid' as often as you change oil.  Hope you can get to it ok, I've heard its a pain in the butt to do.  <P>Good luck.<P>------------------<BR>87 GT, Stock for now<BR><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/Fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD GT's Web Site</A><BR><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD 5.0's Web Site</A>||||reg||1
Z||000003||80's BOY||07-20-2000||03:31 PM||||great info guys. keep it coming. i AM going to use the a/c bracket or at least put it there. there's a blower for sale i'm talking to a guy about that is a ball bearing type which means no oil lines. it needs an oil seal, though. makes me wonder if it's still good and not burned up. can i simply fabricate the ducting and crank it up? why do i need to retard the timing and how? also will i need larger injectors? i have some 19# mustang injectors that didn't work for the 3.1 so i went back to stock. he's selling it for $500 so i really need to know what i'm getting into.||||reg||1
Z||000004||80's BOY||07-20-2000||03:34 PM||||BTW, i have Jacob's ignition so i can buy a control module if i need it. do i HAVE to get a chip burned? also this is an 85 GT so i have no knock sensor, i don't remember installing one on the rebuild anyway.||||reg||1
Z||000005||terryk||07-20-2000||04:03 PM||||There is no knock sensor on the V6's. The ECM supports one but I've never added one myself. I believe it uses the 85 V8 TPI system. You would need the ESC module/filter and sensor (and these are tuned for the engine). <P>If you used a 3.1L block, there is boss on the front side for the sensor, otherwise you have thread it into the block somewhere and one of the water jacket drain plugs seems to be as good as any. Keep in mind that the location is important for two reason. 1) to sense the detontation and 2) to avoid false triggers.<P>Don't try more than 5-6 PSI of boost unless you used lower compression pistons (under 8.5:1 or less). Once detonation occurs you could burn a piston or valves. It can happen before you realize what is occurring. Start low and raise the boost.<P>Get a scanner and watch the O2 voltage at part throttle and WOT. If it drops below about 850mV BE VERY WORRIED! The LED AFR meters are great for confirming the piston has a hole in it already.<P>If you hear detonation or pinging BE VERY worried!<P>If you get the chip switched over to handle boost, you will need a 2 bar map and some heavy calibration changes (power enrichment, accleration enrichment, spark, VE, etc). <P>When under boost, you will be limited to 10-14 degrees of advance at best. With water injection or an intercooler, you can raise that some.<P>Advance and detonation are your enemies.<P>It also depends on how long you want the engine to last and the condition of the engine now. <P>You could go with on the boost controller/spark retard boxes and a fuel line return clamp (ala Vortec) and get it to run reasonable well without heading for ECM calibration changes. <P>When you get into boost, you need to retard the living sh!t out of the timing and raise the fuel flow. Since boost makes the manifold vacuum drop (actually pressurizes it), the fuel regulator will go to max. Clamping the return line will raise the injectors output even more.<P>Also, any vacuum lines to the cruise,etc. need good one-way valves lest you turn them into balloons.<P>What year is the car/ECM?<P>Terry<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 07-20-2000).]||||reg||11
Z||000006||BBBAD GT||07-20-2000||04:30 PM||||Terry has some very good points.  <P>But, I would have to say he's a little conservative with the boost/compression requirements.  I'm running 9:1 on the Stang and its topping out at 14# of boost.  I do have forged pistons, but I've seen hyper pistons take 15+# of boost before, just not as often.  It seems the hypers are almost as strong, but more brittle if the tune is 'slightly' off.  <P>I would say you could get away with just running an FMU and a MSD BTM (or Jacobs equiv) for up to 10# of boost.  I'd take it to a good dyno shop with a wide ban O2 sensor to see where your A/F is, and adjust the FMU accordingly.  Do you homework before putting on a blower, like always knowing where your timing is at WOT & full boost and your fuel pressure (is your fuel pump up to the task?).<P>BTW, I've written a little Basic Blower Tech page for 5.0's, you might want to read it just to get a flavor of what you are getting into.  <P>The ball-drive blowers have been around longer than dirt, and they are no longer being made by paxton.  They do work, just not the best in the world, but for $500, its not that bad of a buy (keep working on him, maybe you can get it to $3-400).<P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/JeffHTML4.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD 5.0's Basic Blower Tech</A> <BR><P>------------------<BR>87 GT, Stock for now<BR><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/Fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD GT's Web Site</A><BR><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD 5.0's Web Site</A>||||reg||1
Z||000007||terryk||07-20-2000||06:19 PM||||Yeah, you bet. If you can get the advance down while under boost and get the fuel up to maintain 12:1 or so, you can crank up more boost. With cast pistons, I'd just creep up on the boost carefully.<P>Weren't the Paxton ball-drive blowers originally for submarine ventilation?||||reg||1
